<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Test-only Logback configuration. It exists for ONE reason: the LOG_EXCEPTION_CONVERSION_WORD
    below. Logback appends a logged throwable's stack trace automatically unless the pattern
    names a throwable converter, and %nopex is the converter that prints nothing.

    Without it, a passing test that asserts a thrown exception still dumps the production
    catch/log/rethrow trace into the build log. JUnit prints nothing itself (assertThrows just
    returns the throwable), so the noise is ours. Measured on this module: 549 stack-frame
    lines in a green run -> 0, with all 285 log records and all 285 tests unchanged.

    ⚠ This repo needs its own file because it is the one place in the stack whose backend is
    Logback rather than JUL — see the load-bearing slf4j-jdk14 exclusion in pom.xml. The
    <java.util.logging.SimpleFormatter.format> property every pom sets does nothing here.
    See "JUL stack traces in test logs" in .claude/docs/build-gates.md of databrowser-meta.

    Everything else is deliberately Spring Boot's own: the two <include>s pull in Boot's
    defaults and its CONSOLE appender, so records keep Boot's layout (ISO timestamp, log
    level, padded thread, padded logger) rather than Logback's plainer default. Setting the
    property BEFORE the include is what makes this work — Boot's defaults.xml declares it as
    ${LOG_EXCEPTION_CONVERSION_WORD:-%wEx}, so a value set here wins and the rest of the
    pattern is untouched.

    ⚠ Two fields of that layout do render empty here: PID and the [corej-cdisc-rest] application
    name. Boot publishes those as system properties when its LoggingSystem initialises, and
    Logback reads this file earlier than that — the same earliness that makes the file work
    at all. Accepted: in a single-JVM test run neither field carries information.

    ⚠ Boot's own logging.exception-conversion-word property gets only PART of the way: Boot
    applies it when its LoggingSystem initialises from the Environment, so records logged
    before that — and by tests that never start a context — keep their traces. Measured:
    549 -> 363 frame lines, versus 0 with this file, because Logback reads this at its own
    initialisation, which is earlier.

    ⚠ Deliberately NOT a test application.yaml: that would SHADOW
    src/main/resources/application.yaml on the test classpath and take the app's real
    configuration with it.

    Safe for RunLogDebugCapture, which taps the engine logger with an appender it attaches
    programmatically and whose level it sets itself — it depends on SLF4J binding to Logback,
    not on how Logback was configured. Boot honours this file instead of its own defaults and
    still applies any logging.level.* properties on top.
-->
<configuration>

    <property name="LOG_EXCEPTION_CONVERSION_WORD" value="%nopex" />

    <include resource="org/springframework/boot/logging/logback/defaults.xml" />
    <include resource="org/springframework/boot/logging/logback/console-appender.xml" />

    <root level="INFO">
        <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE" />
    </root>

</configuration>
